 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The subjunctive is not chosen by the subject",
     "texto": [
      "There is a very widespread false idea: «if the subject changes, use the subjunctive». That is not so. Compare:\n\n- **Quiero que vengas** (subjunctive): the speaker is influencing another person.\n- **Sé que vienes** (indicative): the speaker is reporting a fact.\n\nIn both cases the subject changes, and yet the mood is different. What decides it is the intention.\n\nThe most frequent triggers in a text are:\n\n- **volition or influence**: querer que, pedir que, exigir que\n- **evaluation**: es importante que, es necesario que, conviene que\n- **unidentified person**: busca personas que **puedan** colaborar\n- **purpose**: para que\n- **exception**: a menos que\n- **future time**: cuando **termine** el turno"
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "The three triggers that cause the most trouble",
     "texto": [
      "**1. Unidentified person.** «Busca personas que **puedan** colaborar» does not refer to anyone in particular: that is why it takes the subjunctive. If the people are already identified, the indicative is used: «las personas que **pueden** colaborar son cinco».\n\n**2. Purpose and exception.** «Para que» and «a menos que» require the subjunctive: «para que la coordinadora **encuentre** a alguien», «a menos que las familias **den** su consentimiento».\n\n**3. Future time.** «Cuando **termine** el turno» refers to something that has not happened yet. If the action is habitual, the indicative is used: «cuando **termina**, deja la mesa limpia»."
     ],
